+- [ ] **Step 4: Write the helper script**
+
+> **Post-implementation note:** the draft below used `client_pgid=$!` for
+> process-group reaping, which is wrong — `script(1)` reassigns its child
+> to a fresh process group, so `$!` (the `script` pid) does not reap the
+> inner `zellij attach`. The shipped `scripts/await-dev-channels-prompt.sh`
+> corrects this: it records the inner process PID via a `bash -c 'echo $$
+> > file; exec … zellij attach …'` wrapper (with `SHELL=/bin/bash` forced)
+> and kills that process group, registers the `trap` before `mktemp`, and
+> warns on stderr if the PID is never recorded. See the actual script and
+> the spec's §`scripts/await-dev-channels-prompt.sh` for the corrected
+> algorithm.
+
+Create `scripts/await-dev-channels-prompt.sh`:
+
+```bash
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Wait for the `claude --dangerously-load-development-channels` prompt to
+# render in a launched zellij session, then send Enter to dismiss it.
+#
+# `zellij action` only delivers to a session that has at least one client
+# attached. The launch flow creates the session detached (zellij attach
+# --create-background) and never attaches a client, so this helper
+# attaches a transient pty-backed client via script(1) for the duration of
+# the Enter delivery, then reaps it.
+#
+# Usage: await-dev-channels-prompt.sh <session_name> [timeout_seconds]
+# Exit:  0 once Enter has been sent; non-zero on timeout.
+set -euo pipefail
+
+session="${1:?usage: await-dev-channels-prompt.sh <session_name> [timeout_seconds]}"
+timeout="${2:-30}"
+
+sentinel="I am using this for local development"
+tmp="$(mktemp)"
+client_pgid=""
+
+cleanup() {
+  rm -f "$tmp"
+  if [ -n "$client_pgid" ]; then
+    # Reap the whole process group of the transient client (script(1) and
+    # the zellij-attach it spawned). Silent if they are already gone.
+    kill -TERM "-${client_pgid}" 2>/dev/null || true
+  fi
+}
+trap cleanup EXIT
+
+# Attach a transient pty-backed client so `zellij action` has a client to
+# deliver to. setsid puts it in a fresh process group whose id equals the
+# backgrounded pid, so cleanup can reap script(1) and its child together.
+setsid script -qfc "env -u ZELLIJ -u ZELLIJ_SESSION_NAME zellij attach ${session}" /dev/null \
+  >/dev/null 2>&1 &
+client_pgid=$!
+
+deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + timeout ))
+while [ "$(date +%s)" -lt "$deadline" ]; do
+  env -u ZELLIJ -u ZELLIJ_SESSION_NAME \
+    zellij -s "$session" action dump-screen --path "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || true
+  if grep -q "$sentinel" "$tmp" 2>/dev/null; then
+    env -u ZELLIJ -u ZELLIJ_SESSION_NAME \
+      zellij -s "$session" action send-keys "Enter"
+    exit 0
+  fi
+  sleep 0.5
+done
+
+echo "await-dev-channels-prompt: '${sentinel}' not seen in ${timeout}s for session '${session}'" >&2
+exit 1
+```
+
+Make it executable:
+
+```bash
+chmod +x scripts/await-dev-channels-prompt.sh
+```
+
+Notes for the implementer:
+- The session name is always `claude-<base>-<sid>` with `<base>` sanitized to `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` by the launch skill, so unquoted interpolation inside the `script -qfc "..."` string is safe.
+- `grep -q` returning 1 (sentinel not found) does not trip `set -e` because it is the condition of an `if`.
+- On a real `zellij send-keys` failure, `set -e` makes the script exit non-zero before reaching `exit 0` — correct: a failed delivery is a real failure.
